---
/**
 * A floating navigation capsule, and one monogram that travels into it.
 *
 * Over the hero there is no bar at all. The monogram sits inside the invitation
 * frame, top left of the card, in the handwriting face and in shell — part of
 * the card rather than furniture laid over it. Once the hero's bottom edge is
 * past, the capsule fades in and stays, whichever way the page is then scrolled.
 * Nothing hides on scroll: a bar that comes and goes with direction is a bar the
 * reader has to think about, and this one has six links and a reply button that
 * should simply be there.
 *
 * The monogram is ONE element, not two that cross-fade. It is `position: fixed`
 * from the first frame and never occupies flow space, so it cannot shift
 * layout in either state. Two sets of values describe the two positions and a
 * 420ms cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1) transition on transform, font-size and
 * colour moves between them. Deliberately not the View Transitions API: this is
 * one element moving inside one document, and nothing about it is a navigation.
 *
 * Its hero anchor is pure CSS — the invitation frame's own `--frame-inset` plus
 * a `--mono-inset`, so it is the card's corner by construction and not by
 * measurement. Its capsule anchor cannot be: the capsule is `width: max-content`
 * and centred, so where the monogram lands depends on how wide the row of links
 * happens to be. That one number is measured, from a hidden slot that carries
 * the same two glyphs at the same size, and it is read exactly three times —
 * on load, when the handwriting face finishes swapping in (which changes the
 * capsule's width), and on resize. Never on scroll.
 *
 * Because the slot is the monogram — same markup, same rules, same font-size —
 * the landing is exact rather than approximated, and the two can never drift
 * apart when one of them is edited.
 *
 * Without JavaScript the monogram simply does not travel. `.mono` is painted
 * from the first frame at its hero anchor in both states, and the script's only
 * job is to move it; the slot is `visibility: hidden` unconditionally, so it
 * reserves the row's width without ever being a second monogram. Exactly one of
 * the two is in the accessibility tree, and it is always `.mono`.
 *
 * It was the other way round until the polish pass: `.mono` waited for
 * `.nav-js`. That made the page's largest element wait for JavaScript, and on a
 * throttled phone it cost 3.6s of LCP render delay — for a mark that needs no
 * network and no script to be correct. The cost of the swap is that a no-JS
 * visitor keeps a static monogram in the corner instead of one seated in the
 * capsule, which is a fair trade and was made deliberately.
 *
 * The script below is written tight rather than airy, and that is a budget
 * decision: Astro leaves `is:inline` scripts exactly as written, so comments
 * and indentation are shipped bytes. The explanation lives here, where it
 * compiles away to nothing.
 *
 * What it does, in order:
 *
 *  1. Measures the gap between the monogram's hero anchor and its capsule slot,
 *     and writes it to `--mono-dx` / `--mono-dy`. `left`/`top` are read from
 *     the computed style rather than from a rect, because a rect already has
 *     the travel transform baked into it and would feed its own output back in.
 *
 *     It is measured on load, again once the handwriting face has swapped in,
 *     on resize — and once more at the top of the sentinel callback, which is
 *     the only one of the four that is guaranteed to be exact. The first three
 *     are all bets about when layout has settled, and a load-time reading came
 *     out a third of a pixel short of a reading taken afterwards. The callback
 *     runs at most a handful of times in a visit, it is a state change rather
 *     than a scroll handler, and it reads the geometry at the instant the
 *     travel is about to start — so the landing is exact rather than nearly.
 *     `visibility: hidden` still lays out, so the slot has a real box to read
 *     whichever state the capsule is in.
 *
 *  2. Appends a 1px sentinel to the bottom of the hero and observes it. The
 *     sentinel is below the fold at rest and above the viewport once the hero
 *     is past, so `isIntersecting` is false in both cases; the sign of
 *     `boundingClientRect.top` is what separates them. That one boolean goes
 *     through a single `set()` that moves the capsule and the monogram
 *     together, so the two can never end up in different states.
 *
 *     `set()` is called three ways, and the second and third are both there
 *     because the CSS defaults on their own describe a state that does not
 *     exist — capsule shown, monogram still at the card. It is called once
 *     synchronously from the sentinel's own geometry, which is correct
 *     whether the page opened at the top or was restored mid-scroll and costs
 *     no frame of the wrong thing; by the observer thereafter; and once from a
 *     two-second dead man's switch that falls back to the capsule if the
 *     observer never reports at all. The same reasoning as the reveal
 *     observer's switch in index.astro, and the same measured failure behind
 *     it: an environment that never composites delivers no entries, and
 *     everything downstream of "the observer will tell us" is then wrong
 *     forever.
 *
 *  3. Scrollspy. One observer over the six linked sections with a `-45%` inset
 *     top and bottom, so a section only counts once it reaches the middle band
 *     of the viewport. Results are kept in a map and the first match in document
 *     order wins, which guarantees exactly one link is ever `aria-current`.
 *
 *  4. The mobile dialog. `showModal()` brings focus trapping and Escape from
 *     the platform. Body scroll is locked by pinning `position: fixed` with a
 *     negative `top`, and every exit path — the close button, Escape, the
 *     backdrop — funnels through the one `close` listener that unlocks,
 *     restores the exact offset and returns focus to the hamburger. A tapped
 *     link stores its target and closes first; the scroll happens afterwards
 *     inside that listener, because scrolling while the body is still pinned is
 *     what iOS Safari fights.
 */
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';

const NAV = [
  { label: 'Story', href: '#story' },
  { label: 'Schedule', href: '#schedule' },
  { label: 'Dress code', href: '#dresscode' },
  { label: 'Moments', href: '#moments' },
  { label: 'Q&A', href: '#faq' },
] as const;

const CTA = 'Let us know';
/* One constant for the two copies of this button — the capsule's pill and the
   mobile menu's last item. They pointed at `#contact` while there was no reply
   form; they point at the form now, and having drifted apart once already they
   are no longer allowed to be edited one at a time. */
/* A real route now, not an in-page anchor: the form moved to its own page in
   13b. Still one constant for the capsule's pill and the mobile menu's last
   item, which drifted apart once already. */
const CTA_HREF = withBase('rsvp');

/**
 * Whether this page opens with the hero photograph.
 *
 * The monogram is coloured for what is behind it: `--color-shell` over the
 * photograph, `--color-ink` once it has travelled onto paper. On a page with no
 * hero there is never anything but paper behind it, and it was being painted
 * shell-on-shell — 1.2:1, the one accessibility failure on /rsvp.
 *
 * A prop rather than something the script works out, because the script cannot:
 * its sentinel sits at the bottom of the first section, which on /rsvp is the
 * reply form, so it reads "still above the hero" and puts the mark back into
 * hero colours. This is decided at build time, where the answer is known.
 */
interface Props {
  hero?: boolean;
}

const { hero = true } = Astro.props;
---

{/* Runs before the header is parsed, so neither the burger nor the monogram is
    ever painted in the state that would be wrong for it. */}
<script is:inline>document.documentElement.classList.add('nav-js');</script>

<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

{/* "S & A": the two initials in the handwriting face, the ampersand in italic
    Instrument Serif at 0.8em. The contrast between the two typefaces at two
    sizes is the whole mark — it is not a logo, it is a signature.

    Before the header in document order so a screen reader meets the signature
    and then the navigation, which is the order they are in on the screen.

    It also gives the page a first contentful element that paints immediately:
    Chrome excludes an image covering the whole viewport from being a Largest
    Contentful Paint candidate, so the hero photograph can never be this page's
    LCP, and every other text element above the fold starts at opacity 0 under
    the entrance animation. This one does not, in either state. */}
{/* The way in to the admin area, and deliberately not a control.

    It stays a <p>: no role, no tabindex, no aria-label, nothing that would put
    it in the accessibility tree as interactive. A screen reader announces the
    text "S & A" and nothing else — a guest is never told there is a door here,
    because for a guest there isn't one.

    Three clicks opens /admin. Clicks one and two are silent by design: a guest
    who double-taps the mark must see no hint that anything is counting.
    `data-admin-gate` is what the script hooks; the couple's keyboard route is
    documented in the script below. */}
<p class:list={['mono', { 'mono--bar': !hero }]} id="mono" data-admin-gate>S <em>&amp;</em> A</p>

{/* `topbar--hero` is server-rendered, and the hidden state it names is gated on
    `.nav-js` in the CSS. That pairing is what keeps the capsule off the hero
    from the very first frame: `.nav-js` is added by the one-line script above,
    synchronously, before this element is parsed — so with script the capsule is
    hidden before it can ever paint, and the measured flash of a bar sitting over
    the invitation on a slowly-delivered document does not happen. Without
    script the class is inert, because nothing gates on it, and the bar renders
    solid and complete as it must. */}
<header
  class:list={['topbar', { 'topbar--hero': hero }]}
  id="topbar"
  data-hero={String(hero)}
>
  {/* Reserves the monogram's place in the row. Identical markup to `.mono`
      above, so it is the same width at the same font-size and the measured
      landing is exact — and so that editing one without the other is not
      possible without noticing. Hidden, not absent: the row's width has to
      include it, and `visibility: hidden` still lays out so the script has a
      real box to measure. Never visible in any state — `.mono` is the monogram
      the reader sees whether or not the script runs. */}
  <p class="topbar__slot">S <em>&amp;</em> A</p>

  <nav class="topbar__nav" aria-label="Sections of this page">
    <ul class="topbar__list">
      {
        NAV.map(({ label, href }) => (
          <li class="topbar__item">
            <a class="topbar__link" href={href}>
              {label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <div class="topbar__end">
    <a class="topbar__pill" href={CTA_HREF}>{CTA}</a>

    <button
      class="topbar__burger"
      id="nav-toggle"
      type="button"
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="nav-menu"
      aria-label="Open menu"
    >
      <span class="topbar__bar"></span>
      <span class="topbar__bar"></span>
    </button>
  </div>
</header>

{/* A <dialog> without `open` is display:none, so with no script this is inert
    markup rather than a panel that can be stranded open. */}
<dialog class="menu" id="nav-menu" aria-label="Menu">
  <button class="menu__close" type="button" data-nav-close aria-label="Close menu">
    <span class="menu__x"></span>
    <span class="menu__x"></span>
  </button>

  <nav class="menu__nav" aria-label="Sections of this page">
    <ul class="menu__list">
      {
        NAV.map(({ label, href }) => (
          <li>
            <a class="menu__link" href={href}>
              {label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
      <li>
        <a class="menu__link menu__link--cta" href={CTA_HREF}>
          {CTA}
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
</dialog>

<script is:inline>
(function(){
function n(){
var b=document.getElementById('topbar');
if(!b||!('IntersectionObserver' in window))return;
var m=document.getElementById('mono'),q=b.querySelector('.topbar__slot'),i,place=function(){};
if(m&&q){place=function(){var c=getComputedStyle(m),r=q.getBoundingClientRect();
m.style.setProperty('--mono-dx',(r.left-parseFloat(c.left))+'px');
m.style.setProperty('--mono-dy',(r.top-parseFloat(c.top))+'px')};
place();addEventListener('resize',place,{passive:true});
if(document.fonts&&document.fonts.ready)document.fonts.ready.then(place)}
var h=b.dataset.hero==='false'?null:document.querySelector('main > section');
if(h){var s=document.createElement('div');s.className='topbar__sentinel';h.appendChild(s);
var seen=0,set=function(v){place();b.classList.toggle('topbar--hero',v);if(m)m.classList.toggle('mono--bar',!v)};
set(s.getBoundingClientRect().top>0);
new IntersectionObserver(function(e){seen=1;set(e[0].boundingClientRect.top>0)}).observe(s);
setTimeout(function(){if(!seen)set(false)},2000)}
var L=b.querySelectorAll('.topbar__link'),ids=[],map={},els=[];
for(i=0;i<L.length;i++){var id=L[i].getAttribute('href').slice(1),el=document.getElementById(id);
if(el){ids.push(id);map[id]=L[i];els.push(el)}}
var live={},spy=new IntersectionObserver(function(es){
for(var j=0;j<es.length;j++)live[es[j].target.id]=es[j].isIntersecting;
var c='',k;
for(k=0;k<ids.length;k++)if(live[ids[k]]){c=ids[k];break}
for(k=0;k<ids.length;k++)ids[k]===c?map[ids[k]].setAttribute('aria-current','true'):map[ids[k]].removeAttribute('aria-current')
},{rootMargin:'-45% 0px -45% 0px'});
for(i=0;i<els.length;i++)spy.observe(els[i]);
var mm=document.getElementById('nav-menu'),t=document.getElementById('nav-toggle');
if(!mm||!t||!mm.showModal)return;
var y=0,go=null;
t.addEventListener('click',function(){y=scrollY;mm.showModal();t.setAttribute('aria-expanded','true');
document.body.style.cssText='position:fixed;left:0;right:0;top:'+-y+'px'});
mm.addEventListener('click',function(e){if(e.target.closest('[data-nav-close]'))return mm.close();
var a=e.target.closest('a[href^="#"]');if(!a)return;e.preventDefault();go=a.getAttribute('href');mm.close()});
mm.addEventListener('close',function(){document.body.style.cssText='';scrollTo({top:y,behavior:'instant'});
t.setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');t.focus();
if(!go)return;var d=document.querySelector(go);go=null;if(d)d.scrollIntoView()});
}
document.readyState!=='loading'?n():addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded',n);
})();
</script>

{/* The admin door.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Three clicks on the monogram opens /admin. Separate from the script above
    because it shares none of its state and because this one has to survive
    that one failing.

    Why the element is left alone: adding `role="button"`, `tabindex` or an
    aria-label would announce a way in to every screen-reader user on a wedding
    invitation. The mark stays a <p>.

    Mouse only, and deliberately. There was an Enter-three-times handler here on
    the theory that the couple could focus the mark and use the keyboard — but a
    <p> with no tabindex cannot be focused, so `document.activeElement` was never
    it and the branch could not fire. It is gone rather than fixed: making it
    work would mean making the mark focusable, which is exactly the thing the
    paragraph above refuses to do. The couple reach /admin from a bookmark.

    `document` rather than the element, and matched with closest(), so it works
    for whichever monogram is on screen: the one inside the hero and the one
    that has travelled into the capsule are the SAME node (see the note at the
    top of this file), but this stays correct if that ever changes.

    Silence on clicks one and two is the requirement, so there is deliberately
    no class toggle, no cursor change and no console output anywhere below. */}
<script is:inline>
(function(){
  var COUNT = 3, RESET_MS = 1200;
  var hits = 0, timer;

  function bump(){
    hits += 1;
    clearTimeout(timer);
    if (hits >= COUNT) {
      hits = 0;
      // Astro's base path is baked in at build; this file cannot import, so the
      // prefix is read from the document instead of hard-coded a second time.
      var base = document.querySelector('link[rel="manifest"]');
      base = base ? base.getAttribute('href').replace(/site\.webmanifest$/, '') : '/';
      location.href = base + 'admin';
      return;
    }
    timer = setTimeout(function(){ hits = 0; }, RESET_MS);
  }

  document.addEventListener('click', function(e){
    if (e.target && e.target.closest && e.target.closest('[data-admin-gate]')) bump();
  });
})();
</script>

<style>
  :global(:root) {
    /* The gap between the viewport top and the capsule. */
    --bar-top: 1rem;
    --bar-h: calc(var(--bar-top) + 52px);

    /* The monogram's inset from the invitation frame's border. Comfortably
       clear of the frame's 6px inner rule. */
    --mono-inset: clamp(1rem, 2.6vw, 1.75rem);
    --mono-hero-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.2vw, 2.2rem);
    --mono-bar-size: 1.6rem;

    /* What the hero has to hold its content clear of, which over the hero is
       the monogram and nothing else: its anchor plus its own line box at the
       1.1 line-height declared on `.mono` below. The two move together. */
    --bar-h-hero: calc(
      var(--frame-inset) + var(--mono-inset) + var(--mono-hero-size) * 1.1
    );
  }

  :global(main > section) {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--bar-h) + 1rem);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(html) {
      scroll-behavior: smooth;
    }
  }

  .skip {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 40;
    padding: 0.85rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-ink);
    color: var(--color-shell);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    /* Off-screen rather than display:none, so it stays focusable. */
    transform: translateY(-120%);
    transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  }

  .skip:focus-visible {
    transform: translateY(0);
  }

  /* -- the travelling monogram --------------------------------------------- */

  .mono {
    /* Painted from the first frame, script or no script.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       It used to be `display: none` until `.nav-js` arrived, which made the
       largest element on the page wait for JavaScript: on a slow phone that
       was 3.6s of LCP render delay for a mark that never needed a byte of it.
       Now the hero position IS the resting state and the script's only job is
       to move it. Without script it simply stays in the corner of the
       invitation frame, which is where it belongs anyway. */
    display: block;
    position: fixed;
    /* Above the capsule shell, so it reads as sitting in the capsule rather
       than under its backdrop blur. */
    z-index: 31;
    top: calc(var(--frame-inset) + var(--mono-inset));
    left: calc(var(--frame-inset) + var(--mono-inset));
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-script);
    font-size: var(--mono-hero-size);
    line-height: 1.1;
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--color-shell);
    /* Over a photograph, not over paper. Dropped on arrival. */
    text-shadow: 0 1px 14px rgb(var(--shade-deep-rgb) / 0.45);
    /* A pointer target, and only that.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       This was `none`, and that is why the three-click way into /admin never
       worked: the click landed on whatever sat behind the mark, so the
       handler's `closest('[data-admin-gate]')` never matched and the counter
       never moved. Nothing underneath it is itself clickable — over the hero
       that is the photograph, and in the capsule it is the row's hidden slot —
       so taking the clicks costs nothing.

       Everything else here is about giving no sign that it is a target. No
       role, no tabindex, no aria; a default cursor rather than a pointer; no
       tap highlight on touch; and `user-select: none`, without which the third
       click would select the paragraph and light "S & A" up in blue, which is
       exactly the feedback a guest who double-taps must never see. */
    pointer-events: auto;
    cursor: default;
    user-select: none;
    -webkit-user-select: none;
    -webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent;
    transition:
      transform 0.42s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
      font-size 0.42s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
      color 0.42s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
      text-shadow 0.42s ease;
  }

  /* The travel. `--mono-dx` / `--mono-dy` are written by the script; until they
     are, the fallbacks leave the monogram exactly where CSS put it, so the
     first frame is correct whether or not the measurement has happened. */
  .mono--bar {
    transform: translate3d(var(--mono-dx, 0px), var(--mono-dy, 0px), 0);
    font-size: var(--mono-bar-size);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    text-shadow: none;
  }

  /* A page with no hero: paper is the only thing ever behind the mark, so it
     never wears the over-photograph colours.

     After `.mono--bar` deliberately, and at the same specificity, so it wins on
     source order whichever way the script toggles that class. It has to: the
     sentinel logic reads /rsvp's first section as a hero and turns `.mono--bar`
     back off, which is what made the mark shell-on-shell at 1.2:1 there. */

  .mono em {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 0.8em;
  }

  /* -- the capsule --------------------------------------------------------- */

  .topbar {
    position: fixed;
    top: var(--bar-top);
    left: 50%;
    z-index: 30;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* Between the three groups. The link row sets its own, tighter spacing. */
    gap: 1.4rem;
    /* Sized by what is in it, never by the viewport. `max-width` is the only
       thing that knows about the screen, and only so a no-JS link row on a
       narrow phone wraps inside the capsule instead of running off it. */
    width: max-content;
    max-width: calc(100vw - 2 * var(--bar-top));
    height: 52px;
    padding-inline: 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid rgb(var(--ink-rgb) / 0.1);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background-color: rgb(var(--shell-rgb) / 0.88);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgb(36 28 24 / 0.08);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    transform: translate3d(-50%, 0, 0);
    transition: opacity 0.25s ease;
  }

  /* Over the hero there is no bar. Not transparent — gone: with the monogram
     travelling out of the capsule there is nothing left up there that a
     transparent shell would be holding.

     Gated on `.nav-js` so that the server-rendered `topbar--hero` above is
     inert without script. It is the only rule that hides the capsule, so this
     is the one place that decides whether a reader with no JavaScript ever
     sees the navigation. */
  :global(.nav-js) .topbar--hero {
    opacity: 0;
    /* Waits out the fade on the way to hidden; on the way in the base rule has
       no delay, so the capsule is focusable the instant it starts appearing. */
    visibility: hidden;
    transition:
      opacity 0.25s ease,
      visibility 0s linear 0.25s;
  }

  /* Global: the script creates this element, so it never carries Astro's
     scoping attribute and a scoped rule would not reach it — leaving an
     unstyled div as an extra grid item in the hero. */
  :global(.topbar__sentinel) {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* -- the monogram's place in the row ------------------------------------- */

  .topbar__slot {
    /* Never shrinks and never grows: the one element in the row whose width
       changes when the handwriting face swaps in over `cursive`, which is why
       the script re-measures on `fonts.ready`. */
    flex: none;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-script);
    font-size: var(--mono-bar-size);
    line-height: 1.1;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .topbar__slot em {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 0.8em;
  }

  /* Holds its width and its place in the row, but is neither painted nor in the
     accessibility tree — `.mono` is the one the reader gets, in every state.
     Unconditional now: `.mono` no longer waits for `.nav-js`, so this one is
     never the visible monogram and gating it on script would only give a no-JS
     visitor a second one. `visibility: hidden` still lays out, which is what
     the measurement in the script depends on. */
  .topbar__slot {
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  /* -- the half that only exists after the hero ---------------------------- */

  .topbar__nav,
  .topbar__end {
    /* The delay is the monogram's travel time: the links arrive after it has
       landed, not alongside it. */
    transition: opacity 0.25s ease 0.42s;
  }

  /* Gated for the same reason as the capsule's own hidden state above: the
     class ships in the HTML, so without script it must hide nothing. */
  :global(.nav-js) .topbar--hero .topbar__nav,
  :global(.nav-js) .topbar--hero .topbar__end {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    /* Leaving is not a staged exit — the whole capsule is going. */
    transition:
      opacity 0.15s ease,
      visibility 0s linear 0.15s;
  }

  .topbar__list {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    /* Fixed, not a clamp. The capsule is sized by its content rather than by
       the viewport, so there is nothing for a fluid gap to respond to — and
       compact is the point of the capsule. */
    gap: 1.1rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .topbar__link {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
